Chapter: CapOutsideVocabulary

 Probe: an effect row naming a capability that does not exist is refused.

 The capability vocabulary is derived in Foreword chapter Capability --
 eighteen rows plus a dotted Read and Write for the three families that
 carry a direction, giving twenty-four admissible names. An effect row
 naming anything else has no manifest entry that could carry it, no boot
 grant that could expand it and no policy that could cover it, so the
 type checker refuses the program rather than emitting a binary whose
 manifest silently omits an authority the source asked for.

 This is pinned because three published compliance rows leaned on it and
 two of them were false in the same direction. The CRA matrix described
 an effect-typed Audit channel and a TLS channel requiring a Crypto
 effect row; neither capability exists, so neither effect row can be
 written, and both rows described mechanisms no program could express.
 CDX4001 is what says so, and until now nothing required it: two tests
 mention the code in their prose and no sidecar had ever demanded it,
 which is the condition under which a refusal quietly stops happening.

 Audit is the name used here deliberately, because it is the one a
 reader is most likely to assume exists.

Section: Definitions

  opening : [Audit] Integer
  opening = act
   0
  end

